Run a program interactively as a foreground session.

usage: @run <program> [args]

Launches the program object programs/<program> and attaches it to your
session: the lines you type go to the program's standard input, and the
program's output comes back to you a line at a time. Unlike a verb, which
runs to completion the moment you type it, a foreground program keeps running
and can read your later input, so it can host a prompt, a menu, or any
line-driven utility.

While a program is running, every line you type is sent to it instead of to
the command parser. To leave, type a line of just:

  ~.

That closes the program's input (it sees end of file) and, if it does not
exit on its own, ends it. A program that reads until end of file will also
stop here.

  <program>   the name of a program object, as bound by @compile
  [args]      optional words passed to the program as its arguments

Wizard only for now. A foreground program runs with your authority. Output is
delivered on the server heartbeat, so a reply may lag your input by a moment.

Example:
  @run calc
      Run the example RPN calculator; "3 4 +" prints "= 7", q quits.

See also @compile, help programs, help kobold.
